  <p>"I don't know what program to pick" is the most common thing new lifters tell strength coach Jordan Hoppel. Here is how he answers it, in his own approach and words.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Principles are few, methods are many. The methods I use are dictated by each person's individual situation, and everyone has uniqueness about them.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>The three questions Jordan asks first</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>What are your top three goals, in order?</strong></li>
    <li><strong>What have you been doing leading up to now?</strong></li>
    <li><strong>What is your schedule and availability to train?</strong> Jordan doesn't want anyone committing to a schedule they can't completely fulfill.</li>
  </ol>

  <Media type="image" alt="Jordan Hoppel talking through a program with a client at 5 Star Fitness in Scottsdale" caption="Jordan going through goals and schedule with a client." brief="Photo: Jordan sitting down with a client and a notebook or tablet at 5 Star Fitness." />

  <h2>Match the program to the goal</h2>
  <p>Jordan is quick to say these are rules of exercise science and biology more than his own rules.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strength:</strong> more sets, fewer reps, take advantage of recovery, and don't go to failure often, to keep the nervous system fresh.</li>
    <li><strong>Muscle size (hypertrophy):</strong> similar methods, but you take the muscle into mechanical tension and get close to failure.</li>
    <li><strong>Fat loss:</strong> energy out must be greater than energy in. Keep protein high to hold onto muscle and to help stick to the diet, since protein keeps you full longer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The most common mistake: not really choosing</h2>
  <p>According to Jordan, most people don't really pick a program. They follow random advice from social media or from people with good physiques. That advice isn't always bad, but it isn't rooted in biomechanics or individual differences. A strength coach has seen many archetypes, so a coach's experience is a much sounder approach.</p>
  <p>People pick on vibes. Instead, look at who is writing the program, that person's experience level, and whether it fits your goals.</p>

  <JordanQuote>Coaches who give everyone the same program with nothing individualized: it's cheap, it's dishonest.</JordanQuote>

  <h2>Learn enough to not get steered wrong</h2>
  <p>Jordan also wants people to have some understanding of strength training and exercise physiology, so they don't get steered down the wrong path. You don't need a degree, but knowing why a program is built the way it is makes you a harder person to sell nonsense to.</p>

  <h2>What to do next</h2>
  <p>Once you have a program, give it a fair run. Jordan's guidance on how long is in <a href="/how-long-to-stick-with-a-workout-program/">how long to stick with a workout program</a>, and he covers training frequency in <a href="/how-many-days-a-week-to-lift/">how many days a week to lift</a>.</p>
